 ## Subtopics
 
-### Sample Grid
-Computes the interpolated value at a given position in the grid. It is the high-level interface for field evaluation [S2, unofficial].
+### [Grid Cell Collection via Corner Indices](1518-grid-cell-collection-via-corner-indices.md)
+Focuses on retrieving the raw corner indices for a cell. Choose this when you need low-level access to neighbor indices without interpolation logic.
 
-### Sample Grid Index
-Provides low-level access to actual values stored in the grid at integer coordinates. It is used when interpolation is not desired or for debugging [S2, unofficial].
+### [Piecewise Quadratic Hermite Interpolation](4001-piecewise-quadratic-hermite-interpolation.md)
+Uses quadratic Hermite basis functions. Choose this for smoother gradients than linear but lower cost than cubic.
+
+### [Hermite Interpolation of Scalar Fields](9450-hermite-interpolation-of-scalar-fields.md)
+General Hermite interpolation for scalar fields. Choose this when derivative information is explicitly stored and needed.
+
+### [B-spline Field Interpolation](11066-b-spline-field-interpolation.md)
+Uses B-spline basis functions. Choose this for C2 continuity and smoother results at the cost of larger support.
+
+### [Third Order Elimination (Scaling)](13712-third-order-elimination-scaling.md)
+A specific optimization technique. Choose this for performance-critical paths where third-order effects can be eliminated.
+
+### [Tricubic Interpolation](14963-tricubic-interpolation.md)
+Uses tricubic basis functions. Choose this for highest visual quality among polynomial methods, with higher computational cost.
+
+### [NURBS Spline Representation for Measurement Coordinates](16136-nurbs-spline-representation-for-measurement-coordinates.md)
+Uses NURBS for coordinate mapping. Choose this for precise geometric modeling rather than dense volume sampling.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per sample. Nearest neighbor is a single array lookup. Trilinear requires 8 lookups and 24 multiplications/additions.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of voxels, storing both scalars and gradients.
-*   **Strengths:** Fast access; supports gradient-based shading for surface-like appearance in volumes [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Trilinear interpolation is only $C^0$ continuous; gradients may be discontinuous across cell boundaries unless higher-order interpolation is used. Nearest neighbor is blocky [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** Standard volume rendering where performance is critical or when using simple transfer functions. Use trilinear for smoother appearance.
+- **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per sample for nearest neighbor. $O(1)$ for trilinear (constant 8 lookups and arithmetic).
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of voxels, storing both scalar and gradient vectors [S1, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** Simple implementation; supports both density and gradient-based transfer functions [S1, unofficial].
+- **Limitations:** Trilinear interpolation may introduce artifacts; nearest neighbor is blocky [S1, unofficial].
+- **When to use:** Standard volume rendering where performance is key and high-order continuity is not required.
+- **When not to use:** When smooth gradients are critical and higher-order methods (B-spline, Tricubic) are available.
 
 ## Background
-The method originates from early volume ray casting implementations, such as those described in the CSE-333 Computer Graphics course repository, which utilized 1D transfer functions and computed gradients for opacity customization [S1, unofficial]. The concept of separating grid sampling into "position" (interpolated) and "index" (raw) access is formalized in modern geometry node systems like Blender's, where it facilitates SDF grid modeling and boolean operations [S2, unofficial].
+This method originates from early volume ray casting implementations, specifically referenced in the CSE-333 Computer Graphics 2020 project [S1, unofficial]. It is also conceptually aligned with modern field evaluation systems like Blender's Geometry Nodes "Sample Grid" and "Sample Grid Index" nodes, which provide position-based interpolation and low-level index access [S2, unofficial].
 
